<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-browser</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      Relink the runtime natively. Ava3D calls GL entry points whose signatures are not in the
      prebuilt WebAssembly trampoline table — glClearColor's four floats, for one — and calling those
      aborts the runtime with no exception and no message. Relinking generates the missing trampolines
      from the app's own P/Invoke set. Costs about a minute of build time and needs the wasm-tools
      workload; without it the browser build dies on the first frame.
    -->
    <WasmBuildNative>true</WasmBuildNative>
    <!--
      Give the tab more than one thread. Here rather than on the publish command line, because a flag
      that only some builds carry is a flag that makes two applications out of one: the picker offers a
      different set of renderers, the diagnostics report a different number of cores, and which one you
      are looking at depends on how it was started.

      What it turns on, layer by layer, none of which needed persuading: the wasm SDK links the runtime
      for threads and emits dotnet.native.worker.mjs; SkiaSharp's targets switch its native
      library from the st flavour to mt off this same property; Avalonia's browser backend already
      prefers a managed dispatcher on a web worker and hands it a real OffscreenCanvas, so the render
      worker issues its own WebGL calls rather than posting each one back to the page. Our own code
      needed nothing at all — Environment.ProcessorCount stops saying 1 and SkiaBackend's existing
      CanShareWork gate opens by itself.

      The cost is not size: three quarters of a megabyte, and nothing at all after compression. The cost
      is that a threaded build is a shared-memory build, which pays a little on every access whether a
      second thread runs or not. Measured rather than assumed — two publishes of the same source
      differing only in this flag, Chrome at 1400x900, the CPU renderer, two runs each and agreeing to
      within two per cent:

        scene         single-threaded build   threaded, one core   threaded, all cores
        motherboard   76.3 ms                 77.6 ms              15.4 ms
        stress test   53.5 ms                 55.5 ms              19.3 ms

      So the tax is under four per cent and the refund is five times and nearly three. Both CPU rows are
      in the picker because the comparison should be checkable, not because the answer is in doubt.

      That is a different answer from section 8 of docs/release-journal-12.1.0-preview.3.md, which had
      the tax growing with the scene until it won, and it is a different answer for a findable reason:
      SoftwareThreading shares the banded pixel fill as well as the vertex pass, and at this size the
      fill is most of the frame. What is being shared is nearly all of the work rather than a third of
      it, which is also why holding it to one core is so much worse than never having had the cores.

      And it is conditional on SharedArrayBuffer, which a browser only offers a cross-origin isolated
      page — see wwwroot/coi.js for how a host that cannot set headers gets isolated anyway.
    -->
    <WasmEnableThreads>true</WasmEnableThreads>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The five assemblies that reach native code through a function pointer, kept whole through the
      trimmer. Without this the application publishes cleanly and then draws nothing.

      GlEntryPoints binds every GL function this renderer needs beyond Avalonia's own set as an
      [UnmanagedFunctionPointer] delegate handed to Marshal.GetDelegateForFunctionPointer, and Avalonia
      and SkiaSharp reach their own native layers the same way. Calling one in WebAssembly needs a
      trampoline for that exact signature, and WasmBuildNative generates those by scanning the
      application for the signatures it uses. Nothing references those delegate types statically — they
      are reached through a generic — so the trimmer takes them, the scan then finds no signatures to
      build, and the first GL call aborts the runtime at aot-runtime-wasm.c:188 with an ExitStatus
      carrying no message. The picture is black, no exception escapes, and every diagnostic the
      application prints about itself says nothing is wrong. That is the failure that kept this demo on
      an untrimmed build for three releases.

      The CPU renderer survives trimming without any of this, because it never calls a GL entry point —
      which is why the fault looked like "publishing is broken" rather than "the GL path is".

      The set is deliberately generous rather than minimal: it costs two megabytes against a
      twenty-megabyte saving, and a narrower list would be a thing to rediscover the next time a
      dependency moves. What it buys is the trimmed build, which is the only way to get
      RunAOTCompilation — and that is worth ten times the frame rate on the CPU renderer.
    -->
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Ava3D" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Avalonia.OpenGL" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Avalonia.Base" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Avalonia.Skia" />
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="SkiaSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ava3D.Demo\Ava3D.Demo.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
